 'R6-S46':FT({title:'Training for a (half-)marathon: Training volume and longest endurance run related to performance and running injuries',authors:'Fokkema T, van Damme AADN, Fornerod MWJ, de Vos RJ, Bierma-Zeinstra SMA, van Middelkoop M',year:2020,url:EPMC('PMC7496388'),doi:'10.1111/sms.13725',pmid:'32421886',source_type:'OTHER_OBSERVATIONAL',population_design_dose:'556 half-marathon and 441 marathon recreational participants; questionnaires on weekly training volume, longest endurance run and running-related injuries during preparation and after the event.',findings:'In half-marathon runners a training volume above 32 km/wk and a longest endurance run above 21 km were associated with faster finish times and less decline in pace; higher volume and longer endurance runs were not associated with more injuries.',locator:'Abstract; Results (multivariable models, Table 3); Discussion (limitations: self-report, exclusions, confounding by intrinsic speed) (ssctr-evidence/PMC7496388.xml).',limitations:['Self-reported, associational; possible confounding by intrinsic speed; high exclusion rate; half marathon and marathon, not 10K.'],counterevidence:['Associations do not show that adding volume causes faster times for a given runner.']}),
