const R9_PROBES='Round-9 checks on the current runtime, with a DEV_TEST_ONLY authority bound to hash 18ec48f3... because the real authority has no current record at a new hash: ssctr-probe-delta-policy5-r9.out.json (round-8 sections A-D, G and I-T re-run, with A and P judged against the D47 rule and the round-8 expectation kept for traceability; U: 20 return timelines judged against the declared D49 rule, with the round-8 literal checks kept; new sections V: restarts, moderate returns, goal changes and event-date changes applied by reviews; W: the D48 pending row in the normal and return step-2 branches; X: block credit over 16 weeks and 12 returns close to a 10K; Y: rotation after a return; S: progression stall over 66 cold-start programs and one executed simulation), and node --test (422/422) run by this reviewer. The author reruns in ssctr-dossier/policy5-author-reruns were read as author material only.';
const D50="D50 (R6-PLANNED-PROGRESSION, shared with R6-RUN-QUALITY) r6-program.mjs advanceRun (EASY mode: the reset to the prescribed long run, the long-run step and smallReferenceStep): under a 50-minute long run the runtime counts a long-run step as the single-session increase of the week even when the long-run share cap (or another cap) holds the prescription where it was. The easy step and the quality step are then blocked every week, the easy runs never grow, and the share cap, which waits for easy running to grow, never releases. Probe S: 47 of 66 cold-start EASY programs (3-4 run days, 30-150 min/week) have 4 or more consecutive flat build weeks with every session executed, 42 have 6 or more, and in 35 the easy run never steps in 16 weeks (for example 45 min/week, longest run 25, three run days: two 25-minute easy runs and a 40-minute long run from week 6 to week 15; 60 min/week, longest 35, 10K in week 13: two 15-minute easy runs and a 20-minute long run for the whole program, below current running; a quality runner with quality active for 13 weeks never advances a quality step). The executed simulation shows the same; the R12 conversion leads into it (probe T). This contradicts run.singleRunCeiling.disclosure (the restriction applies to an actual 5-minute step beyond a sub-50-minute reference), run.longRunShareSemantics (the cap holds the long run) and the athlete-facing explanation 'Long run held within its share of the week until easy running grows'. It predates R6-POLICY-5 (identical in FREEZE-1); I did not detect it in rounds 1 to 8. Required: a long-run step that a cap holds is not a single-session increase; that week the easy step and the quality step proceed under their own gates and cadence, and at most one single-session increase per week still applies under a 50-minute reference. Add the probe-S profiles (45/25 and 60/35 with three run days, the quality runner, the all-walk athlete of R12) as regression tests: running keeps growing at the easy-step cadence while every session is executed, and the long run resumes once the share allows it. Runtime only: the declared policy text already states the intended behaviour, so the hash need not change.";
const D51="D51 (R6-ROTATION, shared with R6-NONCLINICAL-RETURN) r6-adaptation.mjs returnAfterPause and r6-program.mjs startStrengthReturn, strengthRowsFor, strengthDose and advanceStrength (the return step of exercises not in use): a moderate or long return gives every exercise in the strength state a return step, including accessories rotated out before the break. Exercises not used during the return never execute an exposure, so they keep returnStep 0 for the rest of the program. The strength state then never leaves the return: rotation is switched off for good, and any exercise added later (for example a substitute) starts at the return-entry dose. Probe Y: after a 14-day return in week 6, standing-calf-raise, side-plank, db-step-up and reverse-lunge keep returnStep 0 through week 15; with EVERY_2_WEEKS the accessories are identical from week 10 to week 15 (the projection alternated every two weeks), and with COACH_DECIDES the week-12 rotation never happens. This contradicts R6-ROTATION condition 5 (rotation on the stated cadence) and the two-step return of R6-NONCLINICAL-RETURN. It predates R6-POLICY-5 (FREEZE-1 and round-8 code give every exercise a return step); I did not detect it in rounds 1 to 8. Required: the return steps apply to the exercises in use; an exercise not used during the return carries no return step once the exercises in use complete theirs (a rotated-back exercise not performed for more than return.moderateMaxDays is re-established by effort, R6-ROTATION condition 8); rotation resumes on its cadence after the return steps, still deferred in establishment and event phases. Add probe Y (EVERY_2_WEEKS and COACH_DECIDES, a 14-day return in week 6) as a regression test. Runtime only.";
