  claims:[C('PP-10','Planned progression must be able to resume when a cap holds one session: progressive overload drives continued adaptation, and in recreational half-marathon runners higher weekly volume and longer endurance runs were associated with faster finish times without more injuries. A plan that keeps every session flat for 6-15 weeks while all sessions are executed with compatible responses provides no progressive stimulus. Progression is a choice the policy has made, and its own declarations say the held long run waits for easy running to grow.','DECISION_SUPPORT','SUPPORTED_WITH_LIMITS',['R6-S01','R6-S46','R6-S21'],'Recreational runners and hybrid athletes with current regular running.',['Observational, self-reported association for volume and performance; the defect itself is a binding contradiction shown by direct probes.'],['Progression is not always necessary for benefit (R6-S01); a predefined program without individual gating also improved performance (R6-S21).'])],
